 "draft": "## Latest JOLTS Snapshot: Labor Market Cooling but Stable\n\nThe most recent JOLTS data (through **August 2026**) shows a labor market that has moderated from its post-pandemic highs, with job openings declining on the month but still elevated in year-over-year terms, and a quits rate holding near multi-year lows.\n\nA chart of job openings and the quits rate since 2019 has been produced.\n\n---\n\n### Key Evidence\n\n- **Job Openings** stood at **7,079 thousand** (~7.1 million) in August 2026 (JTSJOL, 2026-08-01), down from a recent peak of **7,585 thousand** in April 2026 (JTSJOL, 2026-04-01), but still **+2.3% above year-ago levels** (JTSJOL, 2026-08-01).\n\n- **Hiring Rate** was **3.3%** in August 2026 (JTSHIR, 2026-08-01), essentially flat over the past several months, ranging narrowly between **3.2%\u20133.5%** since March 2026 \u2014 suggesting employers are not aggressively expanding headcount.\n\n- **Quits Rate** held at **1.9%** in August 2026 (JTSQUR, 2026-08-01), down **5.0% from a year ago** (JTSQUR, 2026-08-01) \u2014 a meaningful signal that workers have become less confident about voluntarily leaving jobs, consistent with a softer labor market.\n\n- **Layoffs & Discharges** fell to **1,641 thousand** in August 2026 (JTSLDL, 2026-08-01), the lowest in the recent six-month window, suggesting employers are not cutting workers aggressively even as hiring cools.\n\n- The **quits rate has declined ~5\u20139% year-over-year** every month in the past six months (JTSQUR, 2026-03-01 through 2026-08-01), a consistent trend pointing to reduced worker bargaining power.\n\n---\n\n### Bottom Line\n\nThe labor market is in a **\"soft but not recessionary\" mode**: openings remain above 7 million, layoffs are low and falling, but both the quits rate and hiring rate signal that the red-hot dynamism of 2021\u20132022 has faded. Workers are staying put, and employers are hiring cautiously.\n\n---\n\n### Caveats\n- JOLTS data are subject to **revision**; the most recent month is a preliminary estimate.\n- JOLTS has a **~5-week release lag** relative to the reference month.\n- Year-over-year changes for JTSJOL and JTSQUR reflect 12-observation differences in the retrieved series, not necessarily calendar-year comparisons if the series has gaps.",
